{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790430418,"nonce":"wvnXlq3PmVy2m6AWXbwDaaUQR7g=","title":"System.print and interpolation ignore a class's toString","message":"Reference Wren prints an instance through its `toString`: `System.print(obj)` is `writeObject_(obj)`, which calls `obj.toString`, and `\"%(obj)\"` does the same. WrenLift formats instances natively (`format_object` in src/runtime/core/system.rs) and never dispatches, so a user class prints as `instance of Point (Instance)`.\n\nReproducer (interpreter, tiered and AOT alike):\n\n    class P {\n      construct new() {}\n      toString { \"pp\" }\n    }\n    System.print(P.new())        // want: pp        got: instance of P (Instance)\n    System.print(P.new().toString) // pp\n    System.print(\"%(P.new())\")   // want: pp        got: instance of P (Instance)\n\nThe e2e suite only calls `.toString` explicitly, so nothing catches it. Fix: dispatch `toString` for instances (and any class overriding it) from print/write/interpolation, through the same reentrant call natives use; keep the native formatting for built-in types.","files":null}]}